.TH "VCL-BACKENDS" "7" "" ""
.SH NAME
VCL-backends \- Configuring Backends
.\" Copyright (c) 2021 Varnish Software AS
.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-2-Clause
.\" See LICENSE file for full text of license
.
.SH BACKEND DEFINITION
.sp
A backend declaration creates and initialises a named backend object.
A declaration start with the keyword \fBbackend\fP followed by the name of the
backend. The actual declaration is in curly brackets, in a key/value fashion.:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
backend name {
    .attribute1 = value;
    .attribute2 = value;
    [...]
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If there is a backend named \fBdefault\fP it will be used unless another
backend is explicitly set.  If no backend is named \fBdefault\fP the first
backend in the VCL program becomes the default.
.sp
If you only use dynamic backends created by VMODs, an empty, always failing
(503) backend can be specified:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
backend default none;
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
A backend must be specified with either a \fB\&.host\fP or a \fB\&.path\fP attribute, but
not both.  All other attributes have default values.
.SH ATTRIBUTE .HOST
.sp
To specify a networked backend \fB\&.host\fP takes either a numeric
IPv4/IPv6 address or a domain name which resolves to \fIat most\fP
one IPv4 and one IPv6 address:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\&.host = \(dq127.0.0.1\(dq;

\&.host = \(dq[::1]:8080\(dq;

\&.host = \(dqexample.com:8081\(dq;

\&.host = \(dqexample.com:http\(dq;
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SH ATTRIBUTE .PORT
.sp
The TCP port number or service name can be specified as part of
\fB\&.host\fP as above or separately using the \fB\&.port\fP attribute:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\&.port = \(dq8081\(dq;

\&.port = \(dqhttp\(dq;
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SH ATTRIBUTE .PATH
.sp
The absolute path to a Unix(4) domain socket of a local backend:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\&.path = \(dq/var/run/http.sock\(dq;
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
or, where available, \fB@\fP followed by the name of an abstract socket
of a local backend:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\&.path = \(dq@mybackend\(dq;
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
A warning will be issued if the uds\-socket does not exist when the
VCL is loaded.  This makes it possible to start the UDS\-listening peer,
or set the socket file\(aqs permissions afterwards.
.sp
If the uds\-socket socket does not exist or permissions deny access,
connection attempts will fail.
.SH ATTRIBUTE .HOST_HEADER
.sp
A host header to add to probes and regular backend requests if they have no such header:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\&.host_header = \(dqexample.com\(dq;
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SH TIMEOUT ATTRIBUTES
.sp
These attributes control how patient \fIvinyld\fP is during backend fetches:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\&.connect_timeout = 1.4s;
\&.first_byte_timeout = 20s;
\&.between_bytes_timeout = 10s;
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The default values comes parameters with the same names, see \fIvinyld(1)\fP\&.
.SH ATTRIBUTE .MAX_CONNECTIONS
.sp
Limit how many simultaneous connections \fBvinyld\fP can open to the backend:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\&.max_connections = 1000;
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SH ATTRIBUTE .WAIT_LIMIT
.sp
Maximum  number  of  transactions that can queue waiting for a backend connection to become available:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\&.wait_limit = 1000;
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Note that this feature must be used with caution, as it can cause threads to pile up and
increase response times.
.sp
Defaults to the \fIvinyld(1)\fP \fIbackend_wait_limit\fP parameter.
.SH ATTRIBUTE .WAIT_TIMEOUT
.sp
When a transaction is queued waiting for a backend connection to become available (see \fI\&.wait_limit\fP),
this is the time that the transaction will wait before giving up:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\&.wait_timeout = 1s;
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
It is strongly advised to never set this higher than a couple of seconds.
.sp
Defaults to the \fIvinyld(1)\fP \fIbackend_wait_timeout\fP parameter.
.SH ATTRIBUTE .PROXY_HEADER
.sp
Send a PROXY protocol header to the backend with the \fBclient.ip\fP and
\fBserver.ip\fP values:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\&.proxy_header = 2;
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Legal values are one and two, depending which version of the PROXY protocol you want.
.sp
\fINotice\fP this setting will lead to backend connections being used
for a single request only (subject to future improvements). Thus,
extra care should be taken to avoid running into failing backend
connections with EADDRNOTAVAIL due to no local ports being
available. Possible options are:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Use additional backend connections to extra IP addresses or TCP ports
.IP \(bu 2
Increase the number of available ports (Linux sysctl \fBnet.ipv4.ip_local_port_range\fP)
.IP \(bu 2
Reuse backend connection ports early (Linux sysctl \fBnet.ipv4.tcp_tw_reuse\fP)
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SH ATTRIBUTE .PREAMBLE
.sp
Send a BLOB on all newly opened connections to the backend:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\&.preamble = :SGVsbG8gV29ybGRcbgo=:;
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SH ATTRIBUTE .VIA
.sp
Name of another \fIproxy\fP backend through which to make the connection
to the \fIdestination\fP backend using the PROXY2 <https://raw.githubusercontent.com/haproxy/haproxy/master/doc/proxy-protocol.txt>
 protocol, for example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
backend proxy {
  .path = \(dq/path/to/proxy2_endpoint\(dq;
}
backend destination {
  .host = \(dq1.2.3.4\(dq;
  .via = proxy;
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fIproxy\fP backend can also use a \fB\&.host\fP/\fB\&.port\fP definition
rather than \fB\&.path\fP\&.
.sp
Use of the \fB\&.path\fP attribute for the \fIdestination\fP backend is not
supported.
.sp
The \fB\&.via\fP attribute is unrelated to \fB\&.proxy_header\fP\&. If both are
used, a second header is sent as per \fB\&.proxy_header\fP specification.
.sp
Unless specified for \fIdestination\fP, the \fBconnect_timeout\fP,
\fBfirst_byte_timeout\fP and \fBbetween_bytes_timeout\fP attributes are
copied from \fIproxy\fP to \fIdestination\fP\&.
.sp
As of this release, the \fIproxy\fP backend used with \fB\&.via\fP cannot be
a director, it cannot itself use \fB\&.via\fP (error: \fICannot stack .via
backends\fP) and the protocol is fixed to PROXY2 <https://raw.githubusercontent.com/haproxy/haproxy/master/doc/proxy-protocol.txt>
\&.
.sp
Implementation detail:
.sp
If \fB\&.via = <proxy>\fP is used, a PROXY2 <https://raw.githubusercontent.com/haproxy/haproxy/master/doc/proxy-protocol.txt>
 preamble is created with
the \fIdestination\fP backend\(aqs address information as \fBdst_addr\fP/\fBdst_port\fP and, optionally, other TLV attributes. The connection is
then made to the \fIproxy\fP backend\(aqs endpoint (\fBpath\fP or \fBhost\fP/\fBport\fP). This is technically equivalent to specifying a \fBbackend
destination_via_proxy\fP with a \fB\&.preamble\fP attribute containing the
appropriate PROXY2 <https://raw.githubusercontent.com/haproxy/haproxy/master/doc/proxy-protocol.txt>
 preamble for the \fIdestination\fP backend.
.SH ATTRIBUTE .AUTHORITY
.sp
The HTTP authority to use when connecting to this backend. If unset,
\fB\&.host_header\fP or \fB\&.host\fP are used.
.sp
\fB\&.authority = \(dq\(dq\fP disables sending an authority.
.sp
\fB\&.authority\fP can not be an IP address.
.sp
A colon and anything following (signifying a port) is removed from the authority.
.sp
As of this release, the attribute is only used by \fB\&.via\fP connections
as a \fBPP2_TYPE_AUTHORITY\fP Type\-Length\-Value (TLV) in the PROXY2 <https://raw.githubusercontent.com/haproxy/haproxy/master/doc/proxy-protocol.txt>

preamble.
.SH ATTRIBUTE .PROBE
.sp
Please see \fIvcl\-probe(7)\fP\&.
